WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm Stephanie
Wong from the Google

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:02.910
campus in Sunnyvale.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.365
And we're talking baseball.

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:07.668
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.110
Today, we're trying
to figure out

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.740
what makes players popular with
fans in Major League Baseball

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.080
and how you can use machine
learning to predict it.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.185
And joining me, MJ
Jacob, Cloud Engineer.

00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:32.640
MJ JACOB: From New York City.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.950
STEPHANIE WONG: And
Dale Markowitz, applied

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.080
AI engineer here at Google.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.310
Hey guys, good stuff.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.980
OK, so first of all, are you
both baseball fans at all?

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.930
DALE MARKOWITZ:
I'm a baseball fan,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.120
but I'm really a data nerd.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.010
So I think being a
data nerd makes you

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.020
kind of be a baseball
fan because there's just

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.750
so much to analyze.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.260
It's like being
in a candy store.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.273
What about you, MJ?

00:00:53.273 --> 00:00:54.690
MJ JACOB: Yeah, I
would say I have

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.190
a pretty healthy relationship
with baseball for two reasons.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.220
Number one, when I
was a kid growing up,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.140
I used to play a home
run derby all the time.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.709
And nothing's like just knocking
some pitches out of the park.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:08.010
And number two, I love going
to in-person baseball games,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.780
like, being able
to eat a hot dog

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.120
with your drink with your
friends, your sunglasses on,

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.375
it's a beautiful show.

00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:15.660
STEPHANIE WONG: So
let's get to our lineup.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.640
We have some amazing
guests today.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.550
We have Josh Frost, vice
president of Product

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.810
at Major League Baseball.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.700
MJ JACOB: And Kevin Youkilis,
a three-time MLB All-Star.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.070
A player who was incredibly
popular for reasons

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.430
that weren't obvious unless
you were a dating nerd.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.916
[DRUMS]

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.260
STEPHANIE WONG: All right, so
you guys, what do you think

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.720
are the types of data sets
that would help determine

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.430
a really popular player online?

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.610
MJ JACOB: Number one, I'd love
to collect data from TikTok.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.980
Lots of trends coming
out a TikTok, of course.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.203
Number two, the
most popular songs

00:01:56.203 --> 00:01:57.370
that are currently trending.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.710
And then number three,
the most popular dances.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.990
And the reason for this is
think about back in the day

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.700
when John Wall was
trending, right?

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.113
Everyone was like, do the
John Wall-- do the John Wall.

00:02:08.113 --> 00:02:11.284
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.680
It was crazy because while
he was an awesome basketball

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.870
player, I think a lot
more people knew him.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.240
And he was a lot more
popular because of that dance

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.410
and that song that came out.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.410
Dale, what about you?

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.330
Can you fill the rest of the--

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.663
DALE MARKOWITZ: I went on
social media with TikTok stuff

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:25.870
that you want too.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.390
But I also want some
more boring things,

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.270
like maybe how is
popularity measured?

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.750
And like, what sort of users are
more likely to submit answers,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.830
like what is their ages?

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:36.940
What device types?

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.430
So all of the unsexy stuff
that actually can make

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.019
a big difference in the end.

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:42.227
What do you think, Stephanie?

00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:43.852
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
I think there are

00:02:43.852 --> 00:02:45.490
a couple in between things--

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.920
more fun and boring, I guess.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.080
It's TikTok dances
because, obviously, those

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.540
are a big driver for
what's trending these days.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.750
Or does a player have
a orange or red beard

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:56.860
like Justin Turner?

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.530
So there are ton of things
that you can look at and even

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:02.378
social justice issues, like
if they've been really active

00:03:02.378 --> 00:03:03.670
about those things in the past.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.510
MJ JACOB: Yeah, and I think
that's super important,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:06.790
that last point you
just touched on,

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.390
Steph, because at
the end of the day,

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.930
I love whe players
can use their platform

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.410
to make a difference
in the world.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.350
And when they become
popular because of that,

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.932
I think that's an incredible--
two birds, one stone.

00:03:17.932 --> 00:03:19.890
STEPHANIE WONG: Hard to
put that into a column,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.060
but it would be neat.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.300
Joining us now is Josh
Frost with a little bit more

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.240
about how to define an
all-star baseball player

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.180
and to talk about an exciting
challenge for data scientists

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:30.947
who love the game.

00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:32.280
JOSH FROST: Hey, how's it going?

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.780
STEPHANIE WONG: OK,
so Josh, we know

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.318
that there is an exciting new
Kaggle competition and MLB.

00:03:37.318 --> 00:03:39.735
Can you tell us a little bit
more about the details of it?

00:03:39.735 --> 00:03:41.880
JOSH FROST: Yeah, it's
a really exciting one

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.010
because it's pretty
different, we

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.350
feel, from what's been done
before in the Kaggle space

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.030
and certainly in our world.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:51.900
Effectively, we're
trying to ask AI and ML

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.610
enthusiasts to try and predict
what makes a popular player.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.280
So digital engagement is
really how we measure that.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.950
And we're giving
pretty rich data sets

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:06.360
to challenge people to say,
can you divine some signal?

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.530
Can you create an algorithm to
help predict player popularity?

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.110
And we have really
rich data sets

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.350
there, lots of documentation
on what the information is.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:20.250
But ultimately, we think it's
a really unique opportunity

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:24.090
for you to try your
skill sets at making

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.590
a prediction at a
business problem

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.750
that we think about
every day and that lots

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.740
of sports and media
companies alike are thinking

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:32.760
about every day as well.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.225
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:42.165 --> 00:04:43.870
- Wow.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.180
MJ JACOB: All right,
so Josh, I got to know.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.818
What makes an all-star
player in your opinion?

00:04:48.818 --> 00:04:50.860
So I think the question
that's really interesting

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.860
is what makes someone
beloved by the fans,

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:58.090
not just for what you do on
the field that's very creative.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.820
But staying after,
sign autograph,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.550
creating a cool video,
collabing with local artists.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.920
All those things are
really strong signals

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.650
that help define the
modern all-stars.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:11.758
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:12.258 --> 00:05:14.685
DALE MARKOWITZ: So Josh,
talking about players

00:05:14.685 --> 00:05:16.310
that they'd love it,
I got to tell you.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.800
So I'm an applied AI
engineer here at Google.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.220
And I'm like a big fan girl
actually for what you do.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:24.170
And what the MLB
does with data, I

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.020
mean, we're going to enter
into this Kaggle competition,

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.145
like, what sort of data are
we going to have to work with?

00:05:30.145 --> 00:05:33.950
JOSH FROST: Yeah, we think it's
really unique because it's not

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:35.390
just an entertainment product.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.430
It's a performance
product as well.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.960
It's really a
business, of course.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.420
You're getting these
really unique data sets

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.260
combining the differences
between on-field performance,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.980
the business metrics of how fans
interact in the digital space,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.330
both on our core platforms
and even on social as well.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.170
So you'll get some pretty
distinct data sets there.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:00.200
And then what we
call our stats API

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.608
says this huge
range of information

00:06:02.608 --> 00:06:05.150
that allows you to marry it up
with something that you really

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:05.983
want to think about.

00:06:05.983 --> 00:06:08.810
So something that we've
found a lot of interest

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:10.640
in looking at
already is when you

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.060
see spikes for someone on
either the player page views

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.740
or under social
[INAUDIBLE] like,

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.240
did that correlate with anything
that happened on the field?

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.615
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Is there anything

00:06:19.615 --> 00:06:21.680
that you've learned using
all this now combined

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.050
data that was very surprising
that you didn't expect?

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.030
JOSH FROST: Yeah,
I think I'm always

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.300
surprised and impressed
how nationality

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.540
and, like country of origin can
be a really strong influence.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.430
So when Shohei
Ohtani plays, there's

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.080
going to be strong correlations
really throughout Asia.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:42.470
But there are also other, like
less well-known correlations

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.190
on when this player comes
and plays at the stadium,

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.150
he overindexes.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:47.700
Why?

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:48.950
Maybe he's from that town.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:52.735
Maybe he's from that area or
played college ball there.

00:06:52.735 --> 00:06:54.860
STEPHANIE WONG: So being
from San Francisco myself,

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.670
I totally agree that an
origin is a huge part

00:06:58.670 --> 00:06:59.990
of the whole equation for me.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.125
But who would you
vote for in this case?

00:07:02.125 --> 00:07:03.650
JOSH FROST: Well,
I'm super biased.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.020
I'm from Chicago
or outside Chicago.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.570
So I'm always going
to vote for the Cubs.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:12.200
But this year, I think I
have a pretty good case.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.140
Recently, Javy Báez had
this incredible play.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.410
He's called "El Mago" which
means a magician in Spanish.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.230
And he can literally create
something out of nothing.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.168
And he is a top performer.

00:07:23.168 --> 00:07:24.710
So I'll definitely
be voting for him.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.110
But he's also a top
performer because he's

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.680
so dynamic and having swag--

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.250
impossible to define but also
definitely noticeable as well.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.617
MJ JACOB: Or could
popularity be based off

00:07:36.617 --> 00:07:38.450
the shoes or the bling
that they're wearing.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.825
JOSH FROST: We
were joking that I

00:07:39.825 --> 00:07:42.470
would love to create a
metric for the swag chain

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.260
that Fernando Tatis Jr. wears
that like, every time he

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:49.250
makes this mind-blowing
grand slam,

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:54.020
he's putting this very cool,
hilarious, big gold chain on,

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.280
like, no data can measure that.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.640
But like, plenty of data
could measure how people

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.065
react and feel about it, right?

00:08:00.065 --> 00:08:02.450
DALE MARKOWITZ: As we learn
about the more subtle things

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.700
that make players
really valuable,

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.920
are fans understanding that?

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.140
Are they understanding
when a player is valuable

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.210
even if it's not in a Swagway?

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:14.900
JOSH FROST: Yeah, that's what I
love about this era of baseball

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.990
is that there's never been a
deeper appreciation from a fan

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:21.300
or a front office perspective
for on-field performance,

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:21.800
right?

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:25.490
So it's really fun and
exciting to talk about swag

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:26.810
and all those other topics.

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:29.540
Yeah, I've always believed that
all players have a data story

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:33.863
mostly because that helps track
the way that they've evolved

00:08:33.863 --> 00:08:35.780
from someone who was
playing little league all

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.210
the way to being a
major leaguer, right?

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:42.799
And the data story can be in
how a scout discovered them.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.260
It could be an
anomaly or a pattern.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.200
Kevin Youkilis is a famous
example, commonly referred

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.570
to as the Greek
God of Walks where

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.510
he was noticed because he
was excellent at getting

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.670
on base through walks.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:57.957
- Ball four.

00:08:57.957 --> 00:08:59.540
JOSH FROST: Something
else that you've

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.880
seen in the last
couple of seasons

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.270
is that we're using not just
data, but video as well.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.330
We have consumer
data, on field data.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:11.390
We have business data
and industry data.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:15.050
Now, machine learning at AI is
giving us these opportunities

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:19.400
to start gleaning
insights across all

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.810
of these different data sets.

