a real big thing?

00:11:13.405 --> 00:11:14.780
KEVIN YOUKILIS:
It's funny, like,

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:19.310
so when analytics started
coming about in the early years,

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.140
a lot of it was back
behind the scenes.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.390
As baseball players, we just
went out there every day.

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:24.260
We practiced.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.590
We worked hard.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:29.120
So analytics became a
big deal probably, yeah,

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:31.400
right around the time
when I got drafted.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.950
It started to become more
of the norm of learning

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:35.900
about on-base
percentage understanding

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.250
that the key to baseball
is getting on base.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:42.860
On-base percentage is also
a part of getting hits.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.070
So average was a big
deal for a long time.

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:49.160
But on-base percentage
included walks, hit by pitches.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:50.720
And it became a focal point.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.605
The data and analytics is
really good for the coaching.

00:11:53.605 --> 00:11:54.980
There's a lot of
good information

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.920
that is quick and easy,
whereas before, you'd

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:01.070
have to have different
video angles of the hitters,

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.260
trying to assess and see
what kind of body movements

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:03.930
they have.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.390
Now, we have the ability to
assess those body movements.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.923
So when a player is in a funk
or doing something, maybe

00:12:08.923 --> 00:12:11.090
there's something little
that we can take out of it.

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:14.415
[MUSIC PLAYING]

